7592186426265 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426249 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426265 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426253 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426265 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426256 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426265 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426265 1077 "selection" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426265 1078 #edb/ref 17592186426245 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426265 1079 #uuid "bdfbafae-e1aa-5fb7-a7ab-e12c6f01a525" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426236 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426237 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426241 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426252 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426254 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426266 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426266 1077 "selection" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426266 1078 #edb/ref 17592186426241 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426266 1079 #uuid "ce3eb618-d116-5818-bdb6-673400ec83db" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426260 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426261 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426264 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426267 1069 "q-275499" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426267 1071 "A stick of length $1$ is broken at a point chosen uniformly at random along its length. Let the continuous random variable $X$ denote the distance from the left end of the stick to the break point, and let $Y=Y(X)$ denote the length of the longest piece.\n\nExpressing the integrands in the following equation as polynomials, we have\n\n$\\text{E}[Y]={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\text{d}x.$\n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is \n{{field-3}}." 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426267 1072 #uuid "317b9d0c-4dca-5b66-9042-f920e2ca78b6" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426267 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this problem, the break point $X$ is uniformly distributed over the interval $[0,1]$, so the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nWhen the stick is broken at point $X$, it is divided into two pieces: one of length $X$ and the other of length $1-X.$ The longer of the two pieces, $Y,$ is therefore given by\n\n$$\nY=max(X,1-X).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we define\n\n$$\nr(x)=max(x,1-x).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, we express $r(x)$ as an explicit piecewise function. Observe that,\n\n- if $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$, then $x\\le 1-x$, so the longer piece has length $1-x,$\n\n- if $\\frac{1}{2}<x\\le 1$, then $x>1-x$, so the longer piece has length $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ x, & \\,\\frac{1}{2}<x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[max(X,1-X)] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(1-x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/2}+{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is $\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426263 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426268 1069 "q-136896" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426268 1071 "A discrete random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below.  Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+1].$\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426268 1072 #uuid "617cae71-0482-518b-9718-09537ade58e8" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426268 1074 "For a discrete random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}r(x)f(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+1] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}+1\\,f(x) \\\\  & =(4+1)\\cdot 0.5+(1+1)\\cdot 0.25+(1+1)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5+0.5+0.5 \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426266 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426269 1069 "q-136884" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426269 1071 "A discrete random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[{log}_{2}⁡(X)].$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426269 1072 #uuid "8a62d513-53a5-5f10-88b4-24713c5a800b" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426269 1074 "For a discrete random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}r(x)f(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={log}_{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{log}_{2}⁡(X)] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,f(x) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+{log}_{2}⁡(4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{log}_{2}⁡(8)\\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+1+\\frac{9}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426262 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426270 1069 "q-149669" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426270 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{{X}^{2}}{8}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426270 1072 #uuid "9a8266f5-70f1-5e8b-ae8c-803baf5e82ed" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426270 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{{X}^{2}}{8}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-0+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426265 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426271 1069 "q-137052" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426271 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{e}^{2X}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{e-1},\\,0\\le x\\le 1.\n$$" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426271 1072 #uuid "e0495648-4c4b-5e57-86d2-1ae8424c28d5" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true] [17592186426271 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{e}^{2X}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{e-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{e-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3(e-1)}{e}^{3x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(e-1)}{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3(e-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3(e-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534575 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-03d6ff66-b298-5d01-8b05-d5ec795f0ee2" 17592186426235 "answer-05928321-6f6f-5ce5-acf1-3dd017571004" 17592186426236 "answer-140bcf46-7a00-52f5-b945-23c1c8dfa78d" 17592186426237 "answer-1753e041-6891-5669-bd71-7e49a240e47c" 17592186426238 "answer-2d4f012b-2bdb-5642-b09a-a4c7c61052ff" 17592186426239 "answer-36103bd9-fffd-5bc6-b5a5-a409b27b7c1e" 17592186426240 "answer-4504b779-0714-5211-bbac-0fed711fc93e" 17592186426241 "answer-4cf1bca4-dcd3-5a86-81b4-2d4b9f37c001" 17592186426242 "answer-52b345b0-e9ef-5855-ace8-b2531dfe2d44" 17592186426243 "answer-54610ec1-6556-5eea-8174-3ff6615e83d0" 17592186426244 "answer-57054cbf-5a0f-5af1-929e-1cdb78bac5be" 1759218